## 1. Introduction
Shifting from fossil-fuel to renewable energy systems, a process known as energy transition, is crucial for developing
\(2.3\ \mathrm{eV}\).[5] This makes them highly attractive for fabricating tandem solar cells (TSCs), which can overcome the Shockley–Queisser (S–Q) limit of single-junction solar cells \((33\%)\) to achieve higher efficiency.[6,7] On the basis of theoretical
calculation, two-terminal perovskite/Si TSCs can deliver \(45\%\) efficiency, while all-perovskite TSCs can approach \(39\%\).[8] To reach these targets, it is urgent to develop highly efficient and stable wide-bandgap (WBG) PSCs as top subcells in these tandem configurations.
As the top cells, the WBG PSCs play an extremely important role in achieving high open-circuit voltages \(\left(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\right)\) for TSCs. However, the state-of-the-art Br-rich WBG PSCs still suffer from halogen phase segregation and considerable \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) deficits due to the defects in WBG perovskite absorber itself, interfacial nonradiative recombination and the mismatched energy level between the perovskite and charge transport layer. These problems are closely associated with the use of hole transport layer (HTL) materials like poly(triarylamine) (PTAA). Despite thermally stable, amorphous in morphology and well soluble in commonly used organic solvents, PTAA is known to suffer from several pivotal deficiencies.[9] First, PTAA exhibits a highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy level \(\approx-5.1\ \mathrm{eV}\),[10] while WBG perovskites, especially those applied to all-perovskite tandem devices, have valance band maximums (VBMs) lower than \(-5.7\ \mathrm{eV}\).[11] The large energy gap between HOMO level of PTAA and VBM of perovskites causes serious voltage losses at the HTM/perovskite interface, limiting the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of WBG PSCs. Second, the relatively poor surface wettability of PTAA films to perovskite precursor solution makes it difficult to deposit high-quality WBG perovskite films. More importantly, the batch-to-batch variation of PTAA is large if its polymerization were not carefully controlled, since molecular weight and its distribution can significantly affect the carrier mobility, solubility, and film morphology.[10] Such critical requirements make it prohibitively expensive (price \(\approx\!\!\S2000\) per g),[12] hindering its large-scale application.
Apart from PSCs, organic solar cell (OSC) is another research hotspot featuring low material consumption, solution processing and flexible substrate compatibility.[13] Benefiting from the development of new materials,[14,15] interface modification and device engineering,[16,17] OSCs have achieved great breakthroughs with the record PCE surpassing \(19\%\) for single-junction cells.[18] Unfortunately, the most widely used hole transport material (HTM), that is, poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene): poly(styrenesulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS), also shows several shortcomings. The relatively modest conductivity \(\left(10^{-4}\right.\) to \(10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\ cm^{-1}}\) ) may cause the resistive losses and enhance the voltage drop in the interface layer.[19] Its acidic nature is proven to cause corrosion of indium tin oxide (ITO), resulting in the diffusion of \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) ions into the active layer.[20] Last but not least, the high hygroscopicity of PEDOT:PSS also makes it relatively vulnerable to moisture and oxygen ingress and somewhat prone to speed up the degradation of its interface with ITO,[21] which is detrimental to device stability. In this context, developing new suitable HTMs for WBG PSCs and OSCs is particularly urgent and believed to be a judicious methodology to address above mentioned challenges.
The appropriate HOMO energy levels and high intrinsic hole mobility are prerequisites for potential HTMs. In the scope of future high-throughput commercialization, the facile synthesis and deposition of HTMs with low material consumption are highly desired for large-area device fabrication. These requirements can be easily met for self-assembled molecules (SAMs), due to
their simple structures and unique self-assembly properties.[22] Featuring anchoring groups like carboxylic acid or phosphonic acid, SAMs can chemically bond to transparent conductive oxide (TCO) electrode surfaces to form extremely thin and stable layers.[23] Through permanent dipole moments, they modulate the work functions (WFs) of TCO electrode to achieve better energy band alignments between HTMs and perovskites.[24] These features make them attractive to be designed as novel HTMs with commercial prospects, demonstrated by several successful SAMs developed for conventional PSCs \(\left(E_{\mathrm{g}}\approx\!1.6\ \mathrm{eV}\right)\).[25–27] But in the field of OSCs and WGB PSCs, efficient SAMs have been rarely reported, let alone versatile ones that are capable for both systems.
In this work, we report herein a universal SAM-based HTM rivaling PTAA for high-performance WGB PSCs and PEDOT:PSS for OSCs. The SAM structured as 4-(10-bromo-7H-benzo[c]carbazol-7-yl)butyl)phosphonic acid (BCBBr-C4PA) exhibits simultaneously improved solubility and large dipole moment by combining asymmetric conjugation bond and bromination strategies in molecular design. Together with negligible light absorption and lower-lying HOMO energy level, BCBBr-C4PA HTM presents enhanced interface charge transfer and suppressed non-radiative recombination losses. The resultant WBG PSCs realized an impressive PCE of \(18.63\%\) with significantly improved operation stability (over \(99\%\) PCE retention after \(250\ \mathrm{h}\) continuous working), representing one of the highest PCEs for WGB PSCs \(\left(E_{\mathrm{g}}\,>\,1.75\ \mathrm{eV}\right)\). Importantly, these merits are successfully transferred into all-perovskite TSCs to deliver a high PCE of \(26.24\%\) in 4-terminal (4-T) device. The BCBBr-C4PA-based bulk heterojunction OSCs contribute a highest PCE of \(18.84\%\) in PM6:BTP-eC9 system, with good generality observed for PM6:Y6 \((16.78\%)\) and PM6:BTP-BO-4Cl \((17.48\%)\)-based devices. Moreover, BCBBr-C4PA still achieved high efficiency \((16.33\%)\) even increasing the device area to \(0.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) \((0.71\ \mathrm{cm}\times0.71\ \mathrm{cm})\), showing compatibility for making large device. We hope this study can provide inspiration for developing advanced HTMs with broad application prospects.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. SAM Properties
The molecular structure of as-designed BCBBr-C4PA SAM is displayed in Figure 1a, with its synthesis details provided in Supporting Information. The synthesis cost of BCBBr-C4PA is calculated to be \(18.3\ \mathfrak{S}\) per g (Table S1, Supporting Information), much lower than that of PTAA \((2000\ \mathfrak{S}\) per g). For the first time, 10-bromo-7H-benzo[c]carbazole (BCBBr) is adopted as the building block to construct SAM due to its easy-to-modify structure and commercial availability. The asymmetric structure of BCBBr improves not only the thermal stability but also the solubility and film-forming property of the resulted molecules.[28] The electron-withdrawing bromine (Br) atom at 10-position is expected to deepen the SAM's HOMO energy level while potentially passivating perovskite defects.[29,30] Besides, the introduction of Br on asymmetric skeleton helps to increase the molecular dipole moment. To elaborate these ideals vividly, we performed molecular simulations using density functional theory (DFT) calculations. As shown in Figure S7, Supporting Information, BCBBr-C4PA is
calculated to have a HOMO energy level of \(-5.46\,\mathrm{eV}\). Electrostatic surface potential (ESP) analysis was employed to further visualize the electron density distribution. The BCBBr core presents high electron densities (in red color), providing favorable coordination effect with under-coordinated \(\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\) cations.[31] Meanwhile, BCBBr-C4PA exhibits a high degree of charge polarization with the dipole moment being calculated to be \(4.04\,\mathrm{D}\). A deep HOMO energy level and a high permanent dipole moment are beneficial for modifying the WF of TCO electrodes (e.g., indium tin oxide, ITO), thereby achieving suitable energy arrangement with light absorbing layers in solar cells.[27]
The energetic alignment between the studied HTMs and the light absorbing layer has an important impact on the interfacial charge extraction and transport, thus the performance of the whole solar cells (\(\approx\mathrm{PSCs}\) and OSCs).[32] In this respect, we performed ultra-violet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements on the BCBBr-C4PA covered ITO substrate to assess its energetic property. The spectra are shown in
Figure S8, Supporting Information, and a summary of the results is schematically displayed in Figure 1b. The energetic values of PEDOT:PSS, PTAA, BTP-eC9, and WBG perovskite were obtained from the literature.[21] As presented, BCBBr-C4PA \((-5.63\,\mathrm{eV})\) shows significantly deeper HOMO energy level than PEDOT:PSS \((-5.18\,\mathrm{eV})\) and PTAA \((-5.26\,\mathrm{eV})\). This energy offset between the perovskite conduction band edge (or the HOMO level of BTP-eC9) and the HOMO level of HTL is smallest when BCBBr-C4PA is used as HTM, implying reduced energy loss for hole extraction at HTM/light absorbing layer interface.
SAMs with phosphonic acid anchoring groups tend to form an atomically thin self-organized functional layer on TCO electrode by forming strong bidentate/tridentate bonds with the oxide surfaces.[33] To confirm the self-assembly property of BCBBr-C4PA as well as its coverage on the ITO substrate, we conducted X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements. High resolution spectra of C1s and O1s are fitted and shown in Figure 1c,d, while the spectra for the other pertinent elements
are presented in Figure S9, Supporting Information. The C1s spectra are decomposed into 3 peaks assigned to \( \mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C} \) or \( \mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H} \) at \( 248.6~\mathrm{eV} \), \( \mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N} \) at \( 285.3~\mathrm{eV} \), and \( \mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P} \) bonds at \( 286.2~\mathrm{eV} \).[27] In respect of O1s, the peaks at 530.7, 531.8, and \( 533.3~\mathrm{eV} \) are attributed to In–O, P–O–In and \( \scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O-In} \), respectively,[34] confirming the formation of strong covalent bonds between the phosphonic acid \(\left(-\mathrm{PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\right)\) group of BCBBr-C4PA and hydroxyl groups \(\left(-\mathrm{OH}\right)\) on the surface of ITO. Further evidence of BCBBr-C4PA bonding onto ITO surface is revealed with energy-dispersive Xray spectroscopy (EDX) and atomic force microscopy (AFM). The Br atom distribution on ITO is highly homogeneous without any large area segregation (Figure 1e).[35] The AFM height images of HTM modified ITO substrates show higher surface roughness with BCBBr-C4PA than PEDOT:PSS and PTAA, as indicated by the root-mean-square surface roughness (RMS) of \( 2.88~\mathrm{nm} \) for BCBBr-C4PA and 2.37 for PEDOT:PSS in OSCs (Figure S10, Supporting Information) while \( 4.36~\mathrm{nm} \) for BCBBr-C4PA and \( 3.08\,\mathrm{nm} \) for PTAA in PSCs (Figure S11, Supporting Information).
Afterward, we analyzed the optical property of BCBBr-C4PA film by using ultraviolet-visible (UV–vis) transmittance spectroscopy. Since the ITO substrates used for OSCs and PSCs are different, we conducted the tests separately. As shown in Figure 1f,g, the ultra-thin SAM modified ITO exhibits high transmittance over the entire visible region, almost identical to bare ITO substrate. In comparison, lower transmittance is observed in the
range of \( 500{-}1100\ \mathrm{nm} \) for PEDOT:PSS coated ITO while \( 300{-}400\ \mathrm{nm} \) for PTAA counterparts. These results suggest that light absorbing layer in BCBBr-C4PA-based solar cells could capture more photon energy, thereby obtaining higher short-circuit current \(\left(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\right)\). Subsequently, the surface wettability of different films was investigated by contact angle measurements (Figure 1h). For OSCs application, BCBBr-C4PA \((75.1^{\circ})\) film shows much higher hydrophobicity than PEDOT:PSS \((17.5^{\circ})\), which helps to protect the device by inhibiting the invasion of moisture while promoting the growth of upper organic BHJ layer. In the case of PSCs, BCBBr-C4PA \((39.7^{\circ})\) exhibits a smaller contact angle than PTAA \((83.6^{\circ})\), which facilitates the wetting and deposition of perovskite precursor.
## 2.2. WGB PSCs Application
As mentioned above, WGB PSCs are in urgent demand for new HTMs alternatives to PTAA. To investigate the potential of BCBBr-C4PA as the HTM for high-performance WGB PSCs, we fabricated the devices using the state-of-the-art device architecture of ITO/BCBBr-C4PA/WGB perovskite \(/C_{60}/\mathrm{ALD-SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cu}\), as shown in Figure 2a. A methylammonium (MA)-free perovskite of \(\mathrm{FA}_{0.8}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.2}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.6}\mathrm{Br}_{0.4})_{3}\) was chosen as the light absorbing layer due to its suitable \( E_{\mathrm{g}} \) \((1.77\ \mathrm{eV})\) for all-perovskite TSCs. The de
tailed device fabrication processes are provided in Supporting Information, while the photovoltaic parameters of the optimization processes are collected in Figure S12, Supporting Information.
The \( J{-}V \) characteristics of the optimized WGB PSCs with the SAM-based HTM and PTAA are compared in Figure 2b (the specific data of hysteresis are summarized in Table S2, Supporting Information, and the stabilized power outputs are provided in Figure S13, Supporting Information). The reference device using paradigmatic PTAA achieved a PCE of \( 16.82\% \) ( \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) of \( 1.191\;\mathrm{V} \), \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) of \( 17.28\mathrm{~mA~cm}^{2} \) and FF of \( 81.69\% \) ), which is comparable to state-of-the-art one in literature.[36] In contrast, BCBBr-C4PA-based device delivered simultaneously increased \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) of \( 1.286\;\mathrm{V} \), \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) of \( 17.54\mathrm{~mA~cm}^{2} \) and FF of \( 82.61\% \) to render an optimum PCE of \( 18.63\% \), representing one of the highest PCEs for WGB PSCs \( \left(E_{\mathrm{g}}>1.75\;\mathrm{eV}\right) \) (Figure 2c). It is worth noting that over \( 17\% \) PCEs PSCs can be readily obtained with BCBBr-C4PA prepared from its solutions in \( 0.3–0.8\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1} \) concentration range (Figure S12b, Supporting Information). Such a wide processing window highlights the adaptability of our as-developed BCBBr-C4PA to achieve equivalently performing WGB PSCs.
The long-term stability of WGB PSCs is one of the main concerns for practical applications. A successful HTM candidate should not only improve device efficiency, but also endow resultant cells with good stability. To this end, we verified the ability of BCBBr-C4PA on improving the long-term stability of the high-performance WGB PSCs. Under \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) atmosphere in glovebox, the unencapsulated device based on BCBBr-C4PA showed no obvious PCE degradation after 200 days of aging, despite the high Br amount of the WBG perovskite used here (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, we further examined the oper
ational stability of unencapsulated devices under the maximum power point (MPP) tracking with the continuous illumination of AM 1.5G. A big contrast is observed for the time evolution of PCE for different HTMs-based PSCs (Figure 2d). BCBBr-C4PA-based PSCs maintained over \( 90\% \) of the initial PCE after operating for \( 250\;\mathrm{h} \) with continuous power output at the MPP, while PTAA-based devices exhibit a substantial PCE drop even with only \( 2\,\mathrm{{h}} \) of light soaking. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of our new HTM to achieve both high efficiency and good stability in WGB PSCs.
## 2.3. WBG PSC Mechanism Analysis
To gain insights into the origins for improved \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) of WGB PSCs based on BCBBr-C4PA, external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurements were first performed on the whole devices. Figure 3a shows that BCBBr-C4PA-based cells exhibit a large enhancement of the photon response in short wavelengths from 300 to \( 450\;\mathrm{nm} \), which should stem from the negligible parasitic absorption of the BCBBr-C4PA, as described in Figure 1. Consequently, the integrated \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) values from EQE are calculated to be 16.82 and \( 16.58\mathrm{~mA~cm}^{-2} \) for the BCBBr-C4PA and PTAA, respectively, closely matching those obtained from \( J{-}V \) scans (within \( 5\% \) deviation).
Generally, \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) and FF values are closely related to the charge transport at perovskite/HTL interface. Thus steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved PL (TRPL) tests of the perovskites coated on BCBBr-C4PA and PTAA were performed to straightforwardly assess the charge extraction behaviors at the
interface. As shown in Figure 3b,c, SAM-based perovskite film exhibits higher PL intensity and longer PL lifetime (434.84 ns) relative to PTAA counterpart (62.92 ns) (Table S4, Supporting Information), which is consistent with the previous studies on SAMs. This indicates the hole transfer from the perovskite is much more efficient to BCBBr-C4PA layer than to PTAA,[25,27] contributing to mitigated non-radiative recombination. In parallel, the hole mobility of the HTL was tested using the space-charge limited-current (SLC) method with hole-only devices (ITO/HTMs/perovskite \(/\mathrm{MoO}_{\mathrm{X}}/\mathrm{Cu}\)) (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The hole mobility of BCBBr-C4PA is determined to be \(9.75 \times 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), which is much higher than that of PTAA \((6.93 \times 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1})\). The surface passivation effect of HTL on perovskite can be evaluated from trap density \((n_{\mathrm{trap}})\) by using the equation as follow:
\[
n_{trap}=\frac{2\epsilon_0\epsilon_rV_{\mathrm{TFL}}}{qL^2}
\]
where \(\epsilon_{0}\) is the vacuum permittivity, \(\epsilon_{\mathrm{r}}\) is the dielectric constant of the relative perovskite, \(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}\) is the onset voltage of the trap-filled limit region, \(q\) is the elementary charge, \(L\) is the thickness of the perovskite film.[37] The \(n_{\mathrm{trap}}\) was determined to be \(1.43 \times 10^{15} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) and \(3.42 \times 10^{15} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) for BCBBr-C4PA and PTAA-based perovskite films, respectively, indicating higher efficiency of BCBBr-C4PA for promoting surface passivation at the contact interface. Moreover, scanning electron microscope (SEM) images of perovskite films show slightly larger perovskite grains on BCBBr-C4PA-coated ITO substrates than PTAA counterparts (Figure S16, Supporting Information).
Afterward, the Mott-Schottky analysis was further performed to better understand the charge carrier transport and accumulation behavior at perovskite/HTL interface. As presented in Figure 3d, the built-in potential \((V_{\mathrm{bi}})\) of BCBBr-C4PA-based device is \(1.05 \, \mathrm{V}\), much larger than that of PTAA case \((0.93 \, \mathrm{V})\). This means that BCBBr-C4PA-based system shows much stronger driving force for the separation of photogenerated charge carriers, attributing to faster charge transport and reduced hole accumulation.[38] Meanwhile, we conducted the dark current analysis and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements to evaluate the charge recombination kinetics. The BCBBr-C4PA-based device illustrates a much lower leakage current relative to PTAA counterpart (Figure 3e), indicating more photocurrents flow through the device before direct shunting, thus leading to less recombination losses.[39] The EIS plots were further fitted using the software with the given equivalent circuit (Figure S17, Supporting Information) and the corresponding values were summarized in Table S5, Supporting Information. The attained series resistance \((R_{\mathrm{s}})\) and recombination resistance \((R_{\mathrm{rec}})\) values for BCBBr-C4PA-based device are \(22.9 \, \Omega\) and \(45.6 \, \mathrm{K}\Omega\). In contrast, PTAA-based case exhibited a higher \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\) \((68.1 \, \Omega)\) and lower \(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\) \((3.25 \, \mathrm{K}\Omega)\). These results again confirm the realization of improved charge-carrier injection and suppressed charge carrier recombination at perovskite/BCBBr-C4PA interface.[40] Ultimately, the variation of \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) as a function of illumination intensity \((P_{0})\) is studied with the equation: \(V_{\mathrm{OC}} \propto \ln \left( \frac{KT}{q} \right) \ln P_{0}\), where \(K\), \(T\), and \(q\) are the Boltzmann constant, the temperature, and the elementary charge, respectively.[41] In principle, \(n\) value will be close to 1 if trap-induced recombination is suppressed and bi
molecular recombination dominates in active layers.[42] A lower slope of \(1.577 \, \mathrm{kT}/q\) is observed for BCBBr-C4PA-based device in comparison to PTAA-based ones (slope \(=1.786 \, \mathrm{kT}/q\)) (Figure 3f), indicating BCBBr-C4PA can minimize the bimolecular recombination and suppress trap-assisted recombination in the interface of perovskite/ITO electrode. All these results can well explain the higher \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF values achieved in BCBBr-C4PA-based WGB PSCs.
## 2.4. All-Perovskite TSCs Application
Although PCE improvement in single-junction PSC can be possibly expected in the next few years, they will always be subject to S–Q limit. Fortunately, featured with continuously tunable bandgaps and the promising cost-effective mass production, perovskites are attractive for constructing multi-junction TSCs, which can mitigate the carrier thermalization losses to realize higher efficiency at costs suitable for terrestrial PV deployment.[43] Among various TSCs, all-perovskite TSCs hold the promise for high-efficiency and low-cost PV devices as both WBG and narrow-bandgap (NBG) perovskite subcells can be fabricated by low-temperature solution processing.[44] Accordingly, we constructed all-perovskite TSCs by stacking the optimal WBG PSCs as described above and a mixed lead-tin \((\mathrm{FASnI}_{3})_{0.6}(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})_{0.4}\)-based NBG PSC as the bottom cell. The schematic diagram of the device structure is shown in Figure 4a, with the corresponding PV parameters and \(J_{-}V\) curves presented in Table 1 and Figure 4b.
The single-junction NBG solar cells prepared according to the reference (using PEDOT:PSS as HTMs) exhibited a high PCE of \(21.90\%\), with a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(0.879 \, \mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(32.42 \, \mathrm{mA} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), and a FF of \(76.34\%\).[45] On the other hand, the efficient defect passivation in combination with fast interfacial charge transport in single-junction WBG PSCs is transfer into all-perovskite TSCs, enabling higher \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) and \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\).[46] Consequently, our best 4-T all-perovskite tandem device (with a designated area of \(0.0576 \, \mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) generated an impressive \(26.24\%\) PCE, among the highest PCEs for 4-T all-perovskite TSCs reported in the literature (A summary of the state-of-the-art 4-T all-perovskite TSCs is provided in Table S6, Supporting Information).[47] After filtering by WBG top subcell in the 4-T all-perovskite TSCs, the filtered NBG bottom subcell gained a PCE of \(8.98\%\), with a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(0.855 \, \mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(13.50 \, \mathrm{mA} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and a FF of \(77.84\%\). Figure 4c shows the EQE spectra of the constructed TSCs. The integrated photocurrent density of the WBG and NBG subcells are calculated to be \(15.72\) and \(13.16 \, \mathrm{mA} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), respectively, which are in good agreement with the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values determined from the \(J_{-}V\) measurements.
## 2.5. OSC Application
To investigate the potential of BCBBr-C4PA-based HTL for high-performance OSCs, PM6:BTP-eC9 blends were employed as the active layer to fabricate the standard device structured ITO/BCBBr-C4PA/PM6:BTP-eC9/PDINN/Ag (Figure 5a) (with an area of \( 0.04\ \mathrm{cm}^{2} \)). For BCBBr-C4PA-based devices, the SAM solution in methanol \( (0.5\ \mathrm{mg\ mL^{-1}}) \) was spin-coated at \( 6000\ \mathrm{rpm} \) onto ITO glass and annealed at \( 120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 10\ \mathrm{min} \). For PEDOT:PSS-based devices, the PEDOT:PSS layer was spin-coated at \( 3500\ \mathrm{rpm} \) and annealed at \( 150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 15\ \mathrm{min} \). The correlation of OSCs performance with the spin-coating speed of SAM solution was studied, with the detailed characteristic data summarized in Table S7, Supporting Information. The PEDOT:PSS-based control device prepared according to the reference was also constructed to achieve a best PCE of \( 17.93\% \), comparable to the published result in literature.[48] In contrast, the binary OSCs with BCBBr-C4PA deliver over \( 18\% \) PCEs with active layers prepared from a wide range of spin-coating speed from 5000 to \( 8000\ \mathrm{rpm} \). The maximum PCE can reach as high as \( 18.84\% \), accompanied by an significantly increased \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) \( (27.60\ \mathrm{mA\ cm^{-2}}) \) and FF \( (79.30\%) \). The optimal photovoltaic parameters of the OSCs based on BCBBr-C4PA and PEDOT:PSS are summarized in Table 2, with the corresponding current \( J{-}V \) curves displayed in Figure 5b. It is highlighted that the PCE of \( 18.84\% \) is among the highest values for SAM-based OSCs. (see summary in Figure 5c and Table S8, Supporting Information).
Maintaining high efficiency over increased device area is also challenging, but of vital importance for OSC's large-scale
application. Hence, PM6:BTP-eC9-based OSCs with an area of \( 0.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{2} \) \( (0.71\ \mathrm{cm}\times0.71\ \mathrm{cm}) \) were fabricated to verify the feasibility of BCBBr-C4PA to construct relatively large-area devices. As shown in the \( J{-}V \) curve (Figure 5d), BCBBr-C4PA as HTL-based OSCs still realized an impressive PCE of \( 16.33\% \) \( (J_{\mathrm{SC}}=26.30\ \mathrm{mA\ cm^{-2}} \), \( V_{\mathrm{OC}}=0.851\ \mathrm{V} \), \( \mathrm{FF}=72.96\% \)). Apart from high efficiency, long-term stability is another requirement for the practical application of OSCs. For this purpose, we studied the photostability of unencapsulated OSCs \( (0.04\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}) \) by storing them in a glove box under AM 1.5G continuous light illumination. The normalized PCE variations with time are displayed in Figure 5e. After \( 200\ \mathrm{h} \) of continuous photodegradation, the BCBBr-C4PA-based device retains \( 81\% \) of its initial efficiency, while the PEDOT:PSS-based counterparts lost over \( 20\% \) initial efficiency after only \( 40\ \mathrm{h} \) operation. This suggests that BCBBr-C4PA can help to improve the stability performance of OSCs.
To investigate the generality of BCBBr-C4PA as HTM, we further explored it in other representative blend active layers, including PM6:Y6 and PM6:BTP-BO-4Cl (chemical structures shown in Scheme S2, Supporting Information). The detailed photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 2, with corresponding \( J{-}V \) curves shown in Figure S18, Supporting Information. Satisfactorily, PM6:Y6 and PM6:BTP-BO-4Cl-based BHJ OSCs achieved a high PCE of \( 16.78\% \) and \( 17.48\% \) with BCBBr-C4PA as HTL, respectively. Both of them are superior than those ( \( 16.63\% \) for the first, \( 17.32\% \) for the latter) for OSCs with PEDOT:PSS-based HTL. These results indicate the good applicability of BCBBr-C4PA for different BHJ systems.
## 2.6. OSC Mechanism Analysis
In all BHJ OSCs, BCBBr-C4PA-based devices show significantly enhanced \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) relative to PEDOT:PSS counterparts. In order to reveal this origin, the EQE spectra of the optimized binary PM6:BTP-eC9-based OSCs with different HTMs are compared in Figure 5f. Benefiting from the ultrathin nature and excellent optical transparency of BCBBr-C4PA, the OSC displays a broad photon response extending from 510 to \( 840~\mathrm{nm} \) with high EQE values of over \( 80\% \), while PEDOT:PSS-based cell shows a relatively lower EQE in the long wavelength region from 540 to \( 1000\;\mathrm{nm} \). As such, the integrated current density \( (J_{\mathrm{sc-EQE}}) \) deduced from the EQE spectra are 26.78 and \( 26.16~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2} \) for the BCBBr-C4PA and PEDOT:PSS devices, respectively. These values are in accordance with the measured \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) values, and the mismatch is less than \( 5\% \). A similar trend of enhanced EQE response is also observed in other BHJ systems as presented in Figure S18, Supporting Information, ultimately contributing to the higher \( J_{\mathrm{sc-EQE}} \) for BCBBr-C4PA devices.
In general, high EQE curves should be attributed to efficient carrier dynamic factors and optimal film morphology.[49] In this respect, SCLC method was used to examine the hole mobility of these devices. The hole-only device with a structure of ITO/HTL/PM6:BTP-eC9/ \( \mathrm{MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Ag} \) was fabricated and the corresponding \( J^{0.5}{-}V \) curves are shown in Figure S19, Supporting Information. Notably, the hole mobility of devices with BCBBrC4PA is \( 1.23\,\times\,10^{-3}\,\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\,\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\,\,\mathrm{s}^{-1} \), much higher than that of PEDOT:PSS-based device \( (9.60\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}) \). Afterward, we characterized the dependence of photocurrent density \( (J_{\mathrm{ph}}) \) on the effective voltage \( (V_{\mathrm{eff}}) \) to comprehensively evaluate the exciton dissociation and charge collection properties of optimized BCBBr-C4PA devices, and the corresponding \( J_{\mathrm{ph}}{-}V_{\mathrm{eff}} \) curves are drawn in Figure 5g. In detail, \( J_{\mathrm{ph}} \) is defined as \( J_{\mathrm{ph}}=J_{\mathrm{L}}-J_{\mathrm{D}} \), where \( J_{\mathrm{L}} \) and \( J_{\mathrm{D}} \) represent the photocurrent densities under illumination and in the dark, respectively. \( V_{\mathrm{eff}} \) is calculated from \( V_{\mathrm{eff}}=V_{0}-V_{\mathrm{a}} \), in which \( V_{0} \) refers to the voltage at the point of \( J_{\mathrm{ph}} \) \( =0 \) and \( V_{\mathrm{a}} \) is the applied voltage.[50] As exhibited in Figure 5g, the \( J_{\mathrm{ph}} \) approaches saturation current \( (J_{\mathrm{sat}}) \) when \( V_{\mathrm{eff}} \) exceeds
2 V, manifesting all generated excitons in the film are completely dissociated into free charge carriers and collected by electrodes.[51] The exciton dissociation probability \((P_{\mathrm{diss}})\), defined by \(P_{\mathrm{diss}}=J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}\) under their short-circuit and maximal power output conditions, is calculated to appraise the exciton dissociation and charge collection processes in these devices. Correspondingly, the \(P_{\mathrm{diss}}\) values are calculated to be \(97.39\%\) and \(98.07\%\) for PEDOT:PSS and BCBBr-C4PA-based devices, respectively. A higher P value of the BCBBR-C4PA-based device suggests that the application of BCBBr-C4PA realizes higher exciton dissociation and more efficient charge collection.[52]
To further understand the bimolecular recombination behavior of the derived devices, we examined the variations of \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) as a function of \(P_{0}\) in a power law equation: \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\propto P_{0}\).[52] Generally, the fitted slope \((\alpha)\) is equal to 1 if all the generated excitons are swept out prior to the recombination, whereas \(\alpha<1\) indicates the presence of bimolecular recombination.[53] As depicted in Figure 5h, both PEDOT:PSS and BCBBr-C4PA-based devices show a linear relationship between \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) and \(P_{0}\) with \(\alpha\) values close to 1. Especially, a promotion of \(\alpha\) (0.9876 to 0.9902) is determined in BCBBr-C4PA device, indicating suppressed bimolecular recombination and more efficient charge extraction at the interface. To investigate the trap-assisted recombination behavior of the devices, the relationship between \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and \(P_{0}\) is also studied. As shown in Figure 5i, the BCBBr-C4PA-based device performed an n of \(1.024\,\mathrm{~}K T/q\), which is lower than that of conventional PEDOT:PSS-based cells \((1.032\,\mathrm{~}K T/q)\). These results prove that the BCBBr-C4PA can assist to suppress the interfacial trap-assisted recombination and consequently yielding higher \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF.
