## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we developed a versatile SAM as highly-efficient hole transport layer material for WBG PSCs and nonfullerene OSCs. Featuring asymmetric conjugation terminal and bromo functionality, our designed BCBBr-C4PA can realize greatly enhanced interfacial charge transfer and suppressed non-radiative recombination losses. The SAM-based WBG PSC exhibits a maximum PCE of \(18.63\%\) with significantly improved operational stability. These merits can successfully transfer into all-perovskite tandem PSCs to deliver a record-high PCE of \(26.24\%\) in 4-T device. When applied in OSCs, the SAM enabled an impressive PCE of \(18.84\%\) for PM6:BTP-eC9 bulk heterojunction system, with good generality explored for PM6:Y6 \((16.78\%)\) and PM6:BTPBO-4Cl \((17.48\%)\)-based devices. Importantly, the SAM enabled PM6:BTP-eC9 system achieved a high PCE of \(16.33\%\) for OSCs with device area increased to \(0.5\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\). These results demonstrate the universality of our SAMs to act as high-efficiency HTL to rival PTAA for WBG PSCs and PEDOT:PSS for OSCs. This research highlights the importance of molecular design of new SAMs to tackle the challenges in solution processing high-efficiency stable solar cells for large-area applications.
